WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Async TypeScript code runs in an operational world where many things can fail.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:11.000
Network calls can be slow, unavailable, or partially successful.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.740
External services may return stale data.

4
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.080
Browser interactions can be canceled.

5
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.060
Server resources can be exhausted.

6
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:25.720
Defensive async code makes these failure states explicit instead of treating them as rare surprises.

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.040
Handle expected errors close to where they occur.

8
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:39.380
If a user submitted invalid input, if a resource was not found, or if a dependency returned a known conflict, nearby code usually has the best context for producing a useful response.

9
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:48.600
Unexpected errors should still reach a deliberate boundary where they can be logged, converted into a safe response, and monitored.

10
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.880
Timeouts and cancellation are defensive resource controls.

11
00:00:52.980 --> 00:01:02.980
Without them, a request can hang longer than the user workflow, a server connection can remain occupied, or background work can continue after the result is no longer needed.

12
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.640
Cancellation should be part of the design for operations that may outlive their usefulness.

13
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.760
Retries require care.

14
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:23.380
A retry may be helpful when a dependency has a temporary failure, but careless retries can amplify an outage, overload a struggling service, or repeat side effects such as sending a message or charging an account.

15
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:31.000
Defensive retry design considers idempotency, backoff, limits, and whether retrying is safe for that operation.

16
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.320
Logs should help investigation without creating new risk.

17
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:41.620
Useful logs include stable identifiers, failure categories, timing, and relevant operational context.

18
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:48.600
They should avoid secrets, tokens, credentials, personal data, and sensitive payloads.

19
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:54.780
Debugging value should be balanced with privacy, security, and retention expectations.

20
00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.700
Defensive error handling is not about catching every exception and continuing blindly.

21
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:08.280
It is about deciding which failures are recoverable, which should stop the current operation, which should be surfaced to callers, and which should alert operators.

22
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:13.820
Clear failure paths make production behavior easier to reason about and easier to improve.
